hi there steve kaufman today i'm going  to talk about language learning as it  relates to  learning the  language of one's ancestors or the  heritage language as the  as it's fashionable now to call this the  language of your country of origin if  you're an immigrant where do i stand on  it i had two  emails or two uh  messages at youtube i can't remember  which uh one person said that he's  married to a lady from hong kong he's  trying to get his kids to learn  cantonese do i have any advice what did  i do  uh and then another person said that uh  he wishes his parents had insisted that  he learned flemish he didn't do so and  this has cut him off from members of his  family and  sort of challenging me for not being  keen on  the idea of learning the ancestral  language  so where do i stand on this my view is  that to learn any language takes an  awful lot of motivation  so if the child of an immigrant is very  motivated to learn  the language of origin because he or she  wants to talk to their grandmother  grandfather whatever  uh that's great  that's great  however if they're not motivated to do  so then they should just be left alone  uh i don't think that there's any  particular  greater moral  obligation value in having someone learn  the language of one's ancestors the  language of one's ethnic group  rather than some other language  um  you know it's if i take my own case  uh you know my parents were born in the  austro-hungarian empire they were german  speaking  in a jewish community in moravia  the austro-hungarian empire or at least  moravia became czechoslovakia so they  spoke mostly german but at some point  they started going to czech schools once  czechoslovakia was formed so they spoke  both of those languages  uh  they but german was more natural for  them  then they left in 39 before hitler came  in and went to sweden which is where i  was born and so for the first five years  of my life from 45 to 50  1945 to 1950 i spoke swedish  and then we uh 