orn in macau and whose mother  is costa rican  so therefore her the language she spoke  the best was cantonese  but the language of her mother was  spanish  so now in terms of our kids  uh which ancestral language should we we  be forcing them to learn  um  you know as it was i couldn't even get  them to learn french which i tried very  hard to do the more we tried the more  they resisted  it wasn't until my son mark  had the opportunity to uh to live in  different foreign countries as a  professional hockey player but he became  interested in learning languages  so you know i just think language  learning is something you do if you're  interested  if the parents can create an environment  where the children are genuinely  interested in learning the language then  they might be able to pull it off in  many cases they won't and in some cases  they might actually  uh turn the kid off  uh learning that language so and and to  me the culture is not in the dna like we  have immigrants here from uh  from i don't know latin america so  someone who comes here from chile  argentina brazil mexico i mean those  people are also mixed so is the  ancestral language spanish or is it is  it arabic if they're of lebanese origin  i know lebanese origin mexicans i know  uh you know jewish mexicans i know  uh you know there are uh  japanese brazilians african origin  so what's the ancestral language like  how how many generations are you gonna  go back with this  and the reality is that in all  probability in canada within a few  generations all those people will  intermarry and only speak english uh  in canada by the third generation you  know two-thirds of the people  uh have uh spouses  who are not of the same ethnic group and  so english just simply takes over so uh  people get very moralistic about this  you know it's just so obviously a good  thing to learn  you know the ancestral language it's  part of your heritage it's uh you know  diversity and blah blah blah  yeah if people 